the division has undergone several  transformations since it first launched  back in March but the core shoot and  loot setup remains an exhausting time  sync that's both aggravating and  exhilarating whether you're still  spending with its briny loot system or  surrendered to its bullet sponge enemies  months ago the games first paid  expansion Underground offers an enticing  package that includes new gear sets a  third incursion and an entirely new way  to engage the game underground  operations  according to this simple yet sound and  narrative framing the forces you fought  during the campaign have since regrouped  in New York's labyrinthine subway system  and it's up to you to flush them out the  resulting missions play like a strictly  cooperative PvE version of the more PvP  focused dark zone though underground  operations function more like a new mode  than a new area operations take place  away from the open-world setting in  randomized dungeons allowing for  discrete repeatable missions with  adjustable difficulty and gameplay  variables  despite being randomized levels end up  looking just as detailed as the desolate  city above with creepy lighting and  believable destruction throughout areas  seem to fit together naturally - though  I encountered a few strange bottlenecks  and began to recognize certain reused  sections after several hours I was  consistently impressed with the variety  of the level layouts and although  underground tunnels are populated with  the same enemies you fought topside  they've developed some new tricks that  noticeably impact the flow of combat  namely alarms environmental traps and  jammers that disable your special  abilities jamming device effective for  even greater variety you can apply any  combination of Underground's 5  directives to any mission each one  handicaps your team in some way from  disabling the mini-map and directional  damage indicators to steadily draining  your health as you play but all of them  grants you bonus loot and XP if you're  successful it's a clever way to add an  enjoyable optional challenge and extend  underground longevity unfortunately  under ground forces you to unlock new  directives one at a time over the course  of many many hours the one upside to  those hours of padding underground  operations are probably the most  reliable source of quality loot in the  entire game every mission ends with a  boss and a loot chest both of which tend  to yield high-end weapons are better and  every time you level up you're gifted a  cache containing a random item anyone  who spent days grinding through dailies  in order to earn a high-end gun knows  exactly how exciting this is especially  since underground also adds four new  armor sets for the uninitiated equipping  two or more items from the same set  unlocks special stat bonuses in this  case those bonuses boost particular play  styles subtly but smartly encouraging  you to carve out a specific role each  set offers something unique that's worth  working towards which combined with the  addition of weapons and items with a 2/4  gear score makes the divisions loot  metagame alluring again the one annoying  drawback to Underground's generous  reward system desirable vendor items  still require Phoenix credits even  though I had a stash full of high-end  weapons I didn't have enough Phoenix  credits to buy items or blueprints since  there was still no way to sell gear for  anything besides basic currency if all  this talk of gear scores and Phoenix  credits has you confused  don't worry undergroud actually provides  a reasonable reentry  wait for lapsed players you'll be  miserable for the first couple hours as  you try to figure out how the games  changed  but if you overcome this opening hurdle  Underground's gear rewards will catch  you up quickly solo players be warned  though every underground mission is a no  respawn zone  if you die even once you're booted back  to base honestly it's not worth playing  alone like dead all beyond its random  dungeons and added gear sets underground  other major addition is the division 30  version Dragon's nest which despite some  fiery PS is the strongest of the three  this time around the usual grenade spam  is also accompanied by stealthy speedy  RC cars that explode into flames and if  you're under 200 gear score will  probably down you in one shot an agent  on your team requires pay you'll also  have to deal with four simultaneous  bosses who emerge from all four corners  of a rooftop arena all while snipers and  shotgunners consistently force you out  of coverage in a perverse way however  the extreme challenge is a good thing it  forces your squad to work together and  potentially makes all your loot hunting  pay off plus the final room which  requires you to flip switches while  avoiding entire sections of the floor as  they burst into flames is more  thoughtful creative and distinctive than  anything we've seen from an incursion  before it might be a pain but Dragon's  nest is definitely a step in the right  direction  in fact the same could be said for  underground as a whole the unnecessary  leveling system might slow the fun and  solo players may suffer but even agents  diving back in after months away can now  enjoy the new gameplay variations and  pick up and play nature of the 15 to 20  minute operations assuming they haven't  burned out entirely that is
 

